  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 109 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nThe upper trough in the eastern CONUS will lift into the eastern\nGreat Lakes and New England by Saturday, as a negatively tilted\nupper trough digs from the Pacific Northwest into the Central \nand Southern Rockies. This will bring deep surface low pressure \nacross the Front Range of CO and WY and south to southeast winds\n20 to 30 mph across the forecast area. Highs will be cool in \nthe mid 40s. By Saturday night, a closed low is forecast to develop\nover the Texas panhandle with showers developing across western\nKansas and eastern Colorado. Shower chances have decreased to only\na slight chance across southwest Nebraska. On Sunday, just a slight\nchance across far southwest Nebraska in the morning as the closed\nupper low moves east across Oklahoma. The bulk of the showers will\nremain well to our south across Kansas. Highs from the upper 40s\nto lower 50s. Monday and Tuesday, upper ridging will traverse the\nregion and an upper trough along the West Coast moves into the Desert\nSouthwest. Highs will range in the 50s both days, from 5 to almost\n15 degrees above normal. By Wednesday, the upper trough will advance\nthrough the Four Corners and Central Rockies, and could track faster\nand deeper as shown by the deterministic ECMWF. In this case a closed\nlow could move from nern CO into western NE. Shower chances have\nincreased to 30 percent by Wednesday afternoon. Confidence is below\naverage in the track and timing of this system. Highs Wednesday in\nthe mid to upper 50s, which would be from 10 to almost 15 degrees\nabove normal.",
